# Target C-II — bridge-subtracted singleton/rhombic tradeoff

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository base:** `9d7998d86d3e672ba4d1a7f627aadb2ed50aa219`  
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`  
**Status:** proof-only structural consequence awaiting independent audit  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The sharpened correlated all-train theorem forces

\[
\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R
\ge (2\rho+0.00084)D-o(D),
\tag{BS.1}
\]

where

\[
\rho_{rec}=2S,
\qquad
\sigma_R=B_4-4\sqrt{R_4},
\]

and `S` is the number of singleton stopped trains.  Raw singleton count is not yet a
source-level obstruction because a singleton contact edge can simply be an outer
bridge.  This note separates those bridge singletons from the singleton trains that
actually terminate on bounded exceptional faces and pays the former by the audited
bridge-subtracted isoperimetric detour.

Put

\[
C_*:=2\rho+0.00084
=1.479010266430321\ldots .
\tag{BS.2}
\]

## 1. Split singleton trains by endpoint type

A singleton stopped train consists of one positive contact edge.  By the finite stopped-
train theorem, its two train-terminal incidences are precisely the two side incidences
of that edge with either the unbounded face or bounded nonquadrilateral faces.

Write

- `S_o` for singleton trains with **no** bounded exceptional-face terminal incidence;
- `S_E` for singleton trains with at least one bounded exceptional-face terminal
  incidence.

Then

\[
S=S_o+S_E.
\tag{BS.3}
\]

Every edge counted by `S_o` has the unbounded face on both sides.  In a connected plane
graph an edge is incident to the same face on both sides exactly when it is a graph
bridge.  Hence every `S_o` edge is an outer-face bridge and

\[
\boxed{S_o\le B_o,}
\tag{BS.4}
\]

where `B_o` is the audited outer-bridge count from bridge-subtracted isoperimetry.

This implication is one-way and is all that is needed: `B_o` may also contain bridge
edges belonging to non-singleton bookkeeping elsewhere.

## 2. Full-core isoperimetry pays the bridge-singleton population

For the canonical full positive-contact core in the no-container Type-II branch, the
audited bridge/isoperimetric module applies to the **full bounded-face union**.  The
existing near-disk sandwich gives

\[
{\operatorname{area}(U)\over D^2}\to{\pi\over4},
\qquad
{P_F\over D}\to\pi,
\]

while the occurrence-counted outer walk has length `b`.  The audited nonzero-surplus
bridge estimate therefore gives

\[
2B_o\le b-P_F+o(D).
\tag{BS.5}
\]

Combining (BS.4)--(BS.5),

\[
\boxed{2S_o\le b-P_F+o(D).}
\tag{BS.6}
\]

Thus singleton trains that do not touch any bounded exceptional face are not a new
independent currency: their entire first-order population is already paid by metric
outer-walk detour.

## 3. Correlated surplus after bridge subtraction

Using `rho_rec=2S=2S_o+2S_E` in (BS.1) and then (BS.6),

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_R+2S_E+(b-P_F)
\ge C_*D-o(D).
}
\tag{BS.7}
\]

This is the useful structural form of the correlated theorem.  It replaces the raw
singleton/rhombic dichotomy by three currencies with direct geometric meaning:

1. rhombic non-Mantel slack `sigma_R`;
2. singleton trains that actually terminate on exceptional bounded faces, counted by
   `2S_E`;
3. metric outer detour `b-P_F`.

In particular, a near-geodesic survivor cannot hide the correlated currency in a long
forest of outer bridge singletons.

## 4. Continuous rhombic-deficit consequence

Let

\[
\delta_D={b-P_F\over D},
\qquad
s_{E,D}={S_E\over D}.
\]

For any fixed `c>0`, if along a subsequence

\[
\limsup(\delta_D+2s_{E,D})\le C_*-c,
\tag{BS.8}
\]

then (BS.7) gives

\[
\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}\ge c.
\tag{BS.9}
\]

The exact rhombic Mantel rationalization

\[
M_R={T_R^2\over4}-R_4
={\sigma_R\over8}
\left(4\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}\right)
\tag{BS.10}
\]

and `sqrt(R_4)/D -> sqrt(Delta_2)` then imply

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {M_R\over D^2}
\ge {c\over8}
\left(4\sqrt{\Delta_2}+{c\over2}\right).
}
\tag{BS.11}
\]

Thus the bridge-subtracted theorem is a continuous tradeoff, not merely a two-case
pigeonhole.

A symmetric three-currency threshold is especially concrete.  At least one of

\[
{b-P_F\over D},
\qquad
{2S_E\over D},
\qquad
{\sigma_R\over D}
\]

has limsup/liminf scale at least

\[
{C_*\over3}=0.493003422143440\ldots .
\tag{BS.12}
\]

Equivalently, either

\[
S_E\ge0.246501711071720\ldots D-o(D),
\]

or outer detour is at least `0.4930034221...D`, or the rhombic branch has

\[
\liminf {M_R\over D^2}
\ge0.249937509757799\ldots .
\tag{BS.13}
\]

The constants are checked by
`research/verify_target_c_bridge_singleton_tradeoff.py`.

## 5. Why this is stronger structurally than the raw singleton split

The earlier correlated theorem says only that many singleton trains or large rhombic
slack must occur.  The singleton alternative includes outer bridge edges, and those
are a weak source-level obstruction because bridge corridors can be braced repeatedly
by the same diameter chords.

Equation (BS.7) removes exactly that class.  After bridge subtraction, any remaining
large singleton term consists of contact edges incident to bounded nonquadrilateral
faces.  Those endpoints enter the audited exceptional-face closure matrix and the
bounded-face endpoint fan.  Therefore a future closure can attack `S_E` with face
closure/rank or phase-mixing methods without spending effort on pure outer bridge
singletons.

This still does not prove C-II.  The three live escape mechanisms in (BS.7) are each
compatible with current source-level ledgers.  The next useful step is to constrain
exceptional-face singleton incidence itself, or combine (BS.11) with a directional
stability theorem for the now-macroscopic missing-crossing budget.

## 6. Scope audit

- The bridge payment uses the canonical **full core** bounded-face area sandwich; it is
  not transferred to an arbitrary selected harmonic carrier.
- `S_E` counts singleton **trains**, not endpoint occurrences.  A train belongs to
  `S_E` as soon as at least one of its two ends lies on a bounded exceptional face.
- No exceptional-face endpoint is identified with a distinct source vertex.
- No diameter edge is counted once per bridge; the known repeated-bracing obstruction
  remains untouched.
- The `0.00084` correlated margin inherits proof-only AG42 status pending independent
  audit.
- No source-preserving strict diameter decrease and no exact unit triangle is claimed.
